# decisions/spec.md — the top-level spec (`Spec/`)

Owner: SPEC (its own layer since SPEC-3: gates/OWNERS rows `Spec/` and `decisions/spec.md`, prefix `spec:`;
the first four landings went in as `root:`). Design:
`redregg/work/promo/TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md`. Format per decisions/README.md.

## SPEC-1 Claims are predicates on a record of the deployed entry points (2026-10-26)

**Decision.** `Spec/System.lean` defines `System D`, a record whose fields are the decisions the
binaries run (`receive`, `recordedBy`, `admit`, `refuse`, `submit`, `follow`, `settle`, `handle`) plus
the context they read through (`X`, `dc`, `B`); `deployed d` fills each with the deployed definition
(`B` exactly as `Host/Follow.lean` builds it from the Store's `worldDigest`). Every claim is
`System D → Prop`; the top theorem is `Claim (deployed d)`; the floor is `¬ Claim trivial` for a
trivial system that is `deployed d` with one field replaced.

**Alternatives.** (a) An abstract vat the kernel refines (GATES' reading): a second machine beside
`World.admit`, a twin by law 1, and its own non-vacuity needs the same floors. (b) Claims stated
directly over the deployed definitions: no field to swap, so no floor can be stated; the kernel's
transition is a fixed `def` (`World.admit`, `receive`), not a `Ctx` field. (c) Claims parameterised by
the whole `Ctx`: `Ctx` varies the codec, `verify` and the program, not the kernel's decisions.

**Why.** It is the shape the four `host_step_folds_tooth_*` already use (one arm of a record
replaced, the statement refused), generalised; it costs one record and no second semantics.

**CRITIQUE.** The fields fix the granularity of every floor: a trivial system can swap `receive` as a
whole, not one check inside it (the spec's `openKernel`, "`ownerAuthority` returns the actor", is not
expressible without copying `receive`). `deployed` can drift from what the binaries call: it is a
second list of the wiring `Host/Follow.lean` and `Host/Serve.lean` perform, checked by nothing but
reading; a binary that called a different `step` would leave `deployed` true and the claims about
the wrong thing.

**META-CRITIQUE.** I took KERNEL's side of the GATES/KERNEL debate because the design document did,
and the design document did because it is cheaper to reuse names than to write a model. The drift
risk of `deployed` is the same risk the readers named in `host_run_audits` (a theorem about a
re-deriver nobody ran); the remedy (the binaries' `main` taking a `System`) is not taken here.

**Status.** open.

## SPEC-2 The layout the closure gates force: the trusted reading lives under `Spec/Proofs/` (2026-10-26)

**Decision.** `Spec/System.lean` (a def-module: the record and `deployed`, no statement);
`Spec/Proofs/Claims.lean` (every claim, and the vocabulary they share: `Decided`, `Audited`, `Start`,
`keyFaithful`, `Moves`, `Reach`); `Spec/Proofs/Floor.lean` (the trivial systems, their refutations, the
deployed run); `Spec/Proofs/Bridge.lean` and `Spec/Proofs/Deployed.lean` (proofs). The trusted
reading is System + Claims + Floor (≈ 520 lines); Bridge and Deployed are checked, not read.

**Alternatives.** TOPLEVEL-SPEC §12's `Spec/Claims.lean` beside `Spec/Floor.lean`: red at closure C3 (a
def-module exporting a statement) and C4 (importing `Kernel.Proofs.Rederive`, where `Honest` lives).
Amending law 8 so a spec tier may hold statements outside `Proofs` (the design's own proposal).

**Why.** The gates are right that a statement is not a definition; the path name is the only cost.

**CRITIQUE.** A reader looking for "the spec" finds it in a directory named `Proofs`, beside the
proofs it is meant to be read without. `Floor.lean` must hold its deployed run's defs `private` (C3),
so the run cannot be named from outside the file.

**META-CRITIQUE.** Choosing not to amend law 8 is choosing the path of no argument with GATES; the
design said the amendment was owed. If a second spec-tier consumer appears, amend it then.

**Status.** open.

## SPEC-3 `Decided`/`Audited`: the kernel's honesty with the kernel a parameter (2026-10-26)

**Decision.** `Decided S` is `Receive.Honest` with `receive`, `recordedBy`, `admit`, `refuse` read from
`S`; `Audited S w l w' := S.fold w l = .ok w' ∧ Decided S w l`. `Spec.Proofs.Bridge` proves
`audited_iff`: for any `S` whose four kernel fields are the kernel's, `Audited` is exactly
`World.fold ∧ Honest`, so every theorem over the kernel's audit is a theorem about `Audited deployed`.

**Alternatives.** (a) Claims over `Receive.Honest X` directly: then a kernel that admits everything
cannot be plugged in and INTEGRITY has no floor (the apex argument of TOPLEVEL-SPEC §2.FLOOR needs
`OneHistory` to hold of that kernel and `Integrity` to fail of it). (b) Make `Kernel.Receive.Honest`
itself the instance `Decided (deployed …)`: a cross-layer change under every audit theorem.
/-
# Protocol.Foreign — a receipt from another World, checked against a head this World holds (E5)

`check E dc` is the deployed `Lang.ReceiptCheck` (`Host.Deployed.X` passes it to `Lang.program`): the
point's cell must be an object of `Std.headPackage` (`Lang.headView`, else `notHead`) holding an
`at` head (a `forked` one checks nothing: `forkedHead`; the head's chain is for comparing folds,
PROTOCOL-75, and the check ignores it); the receipt's bytes must decode
(`badReceipt`); `Receipt.check` against the head's height and roots must answer `committed`
(`headMismatch` / `pathMismatch` / `forgotten` by the check's own names; `refused` and `absent`
are `notCommitted`: acting on them needs the call's window, which the receipt does not carry).
The column then shows the committed height, turn digest and transaction id.

What it means is `Proofs.Foreign.foreign_receipt_sound`: under the named premise that the held head
is the head of a fold of A from A's genesis (head freshness: B holds A head, not THE head), the
transaction was admitted in that fold at that height, or the hash collides among a listed set.
-/
import Protocol.Receipt
import Lang.StdHead

namespace Nanodregg.Protocol.Foreign

open Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive Nanodregg.Codec

variable {D : Type} [Ord D] [DecidableEq D]

/-- `Std.headPackage`'s canonical bytes (empty if it had none, which `headPackage_encodes` refutes). -/
def headPackageBytes : ByteArray := (Lang.Wire.encodePackage Lang.Std.headPackage).getD .empty

/-- The deployed id of `Std.headPackage`: its package cell's content digest (`install`, KERNEL-3). -/
def headPackageId (E : Env D) : D :=
  romIdOf E { kind := kindPackage, discipline := .rom, slots := [(0, headPackageBytes)] }

/-- THE deployed receipt check. -/
def check (E : Env D) (dc : Stream D) : Lang.ReceiptCheck := fun cell rb =>
  let digestOf (b : ByteArray) : Option D := (dc.decode b).toOption
  match Lang.headView digestOf (E.bytes (headPackageId E)) cell with
  | none => .error "notHead"
  | some (.forked _) => .error "forkedHead"
  | some (.at _ _ height rootsB _) =>
    match digestOf rootsB, Receipt.ofBytes E dc rb with
    | none, _ => .error "notHead"
    | _, none => .error "badReceipt"
    | some roots, some r =>
      match Receipt.check E height roots r.txId 0 r with
      | .ok (.committed h td) => .ok ⟨h, E.bytes td, E.bytes r.txId⟩
      | .ok _ => .error "notCommitted"
      | .error e => .error e.name

end Nanodregg.Protocol.Foreign
/-
# Ledger.Proofs.EscrowInvokes — the escrow pays out at most once over the history the World admits

`escrow_runs_one_payout` (Ledger.Proofs.EscrowRun) bounds the payouts of a `Runs` chain: runs the
receiver commits, each next run reading slots that hold, key by key, what the run before it wrote.
Here the chain is the World's: an `ObjectHistory` around one escrow object (KERNEL's
`Kernel.Proofs.Logic.History`: every step of the World, as the object sees it; a step that leaves
it alone records nothing, a step that judges it records what the judge saw). KERNEL lifts it once
(`object_history_chain`): the judged steps are a `Std.Chain` of the escrow law's admitted steps from
the object's state, and each invoke's calls are its run's (`object_history_runs`). So:

* `escrow_invokes_one_payout`: along any `ObjectHistory` of an object whose every judged step was
  judged by the escrow package (read as `escrow` in the step's view), at most one call the invokes
  return is a payout, at a lawful `Ctx` whose program is the language over its own codec:
  `payoutCalls_le_one`, `chain_count` of the steps into a settled state. `escrow_invokes_deployed`
  at the node's (`Host.Deployed.X`), premises only the history and its package reading.
* Completeness: an honest folded log is such a history (`object_history_of_honest`), and a history files no
  invoke of the object as a step that left it alone (`ObjectStep.keep` says "no invoke"; `other`
  and `frame` exclude a touch of it), so the count is of every invoke the log holds.

The history covers every invoke of the object, signed or NESTED in another object's call tree
(`ObjectStep.nested`), and every other turn and recorded refusal (the hand-rolled `Invokes`, which
covered only signed top-level invokes, is deleted: it was this instance). The teeth are
`Ledger.Proofs.EscrowWitness`'s: `escrow_link_bites` (without the next-state link an admitted
payout repeats) and `escrow_second_release_refused` (without the judge a release from `released`
pays again). The premises hold together, with the bound attained, through the real `receive` and
`World.admit`: `Ledger.Proofs.EscrowAdmitted.escrow_history_witnessed` (a deposit through a grant
capped at the escrow's amount, then a release; LEDGER-18).
-/
import Ledger.Proofs.EscrowWitness
import Kernel.Proofs.Logic.History

namespace Nanodregg.Ledger.Proofs

open Nanodregg.Lang
open Nanodregg.Kernel Nanodregg.Kernel.Receive
open Nanodregg.Ledger.Escrow

section
variable {D : Type} [Ord D] [DecidableEq D]

omit [Ord D] [DecidableEq D] in
/-- The judged steps of a history, paired with the chain's steps the lift reads them as. -/
private theorem pair_steps {digestOf : ByteArray → Option D} {bytesOf : D → ByteArray} {row : List (Label × Ty)} :
    ∀ (tr : List (Pinned D × Option D × List D × Req D × Slots × Slots × List (Body D)))
      (steps : List (Data × Data)),
      tr.map (fun e => (reqData bytesOf e.2.2.2.1, readState digestOf row e.2.2.2.2.2.1)) =
        steps.map (fun s => (s.1, .ok s.2)) →
      ∃ ps : List (List (Body D) × (Data × Data)), ps.map Prod.fst = tr.map (fun e => e.2.2.2.2.2.2) ∧
        ps.map Prod.snd = steps ∧
        ∀ x ∈ ps, ∃ e ∈ tr, x.1 = e.2.2.2.2.2.2 ∧ readState digestOf row e.2.2.2.2.2.1 = .ok x.2.2
  | [], [], _ => ⟨[], rfl, rfl, by simp⟩
  | [], _ :: _, h => by simp at h
  | _ :: _, [], h => by simp at h
  | e :: tr, s :: steps, h => by
      simp only [List.map_cons, List.cons.injEq, Prod.mk.injEq] at h
      obtain ⟨⟨-, hn⟩, h⟩ := h
      obtain ⟨ps, h1, h2, h3⟩ := pair_steps tr steps h
      refine ⟨(e.2.2.2.2.2.2, s) :: ps, by simp [h1], by simp [h2], fun x hx => ?_⟩
      rcases List.mem_cons.mp hx with rfl | hx
      · exact ⟨e, List.mem_cons_self .., rfl, hn⟩
      · obtain ⟨e', he', h4, h5⟩ := h3 x hx
        exact ⟨e', List.mem_cons_of_mem _ he', h4, h5⟩

/-- **The escrow pays out at most once over the history the World admits.** Along any
`ObjectHistory` of one object (its signed and nested invokes, writes, law changes, every other turn
and recorded refusal) whose every judged step was judged by the package `pkg`, read as `escrow` in
that step's view, at most one call the invokes return is a payout, at a lawful `Ctx` whose program
is the language over its own codec. -/
theorem escrow_invokes_one_payout {rc : Lang.ReceiptCheck} {X : Ctx D} (hX : X.Lawful)
    (hprog : X.program = Lang.program X.env.bytes X.digestOf rc) {obj pkg : D} {w w' : World D}
    {tr : List (Pinned D × Option D × List D × Req D × Slots × Slots × List (Body D))}
    (h : ObjectHistory X obj w w' tr)
    (hpkg : ∀ e ∈ tr, e.2.1 = some pkg ∧ readPackage e.1 pkg = .ok escrow) :
    payoutCalls (tr.map fun e => e.2.2.2.2.2.2) ≤ 1 := by
  by_cases hne : tr = []
  · subst hne; simp [payoutCalls]
  have hin : ∀ e ∈ tr, pkg ∈ e.2.1.toList ++ e.2.2.1 ∧ readPackage e.1 pkg = .ok escrow := fun e he => by
    obtain ⟨h1, h2⟩ := hpkg e he
    exact ⟨by rw [h1]; simp, h2⟩
  obtain ⟨cell, o, hl, ho⟩ := object_history_start hX hprog h hin hne
  obtain ⟨steps, hc, hmap⟩ := object_history_chain hX hprog escrow_body (by rw [escrow_laws]; simp)
    escrow_field escrow_law_isLaw escrow_law_noRef h hin hl ho
  obtain ⟨ps, hfst, hsnd, hpair⟩ := pair_steps tr steps hmap
  have hruns := object_history_runs h
  rw [← hfst]
  have hca : Std.Chain (Std.Admits [] law) o (ps.map Prod.snd) := by
    rw [hsnd]
    exact Std.chain_mono (R := Std.Step [] law) (fun _ _ _ hs => hs.1) hc
  refine payoutCalls_le_one ps hca (fun x hx => ?_)
  obtain ⟨e, he, hcalls, hn⟩ := hpair x hx
  obtain ⟨rv, method, args, fuel, rows, used, -, hrun⟩ := (hruns e he).2 pkg (hpkg e he).1
  rw [hcalls]
  exact runCalls_payout (escrow_program_calls hX hprog (hpkg e he).2 hrun) hn

end

/-- **Deployed**: along any history the node (`Host.Deployed.X`) admits around one object whose
every judged step was judged by a package read as `escrow`, at most one returned call is a payout;
premises: the history and that package reading, the `Ctx` discharged (`deployed_ctx_lawful`, `rfl`). -/
theorem escrow_invokes_deployed {rc : Lang.ReceiptCheck} {obj pkg : Codec.Digest} {w w' : World Codec.Digest}
    {tr : List (Pinned Codec.Digest × Option Codec.Digest × List Codec.Digest × Req Codec.Digest × Slots × Slots ×
      List (Body Codec.Digest))}
    (h : ObjectHistory (Codec.Deployed.ctx (Lang.program Codec.Digest.bytes Codec.Digest.ofBytes? rc)) obj w w' tr)
    (hpkg : ∀ e ∈ tr, e.2.1 = some pkg ∧ readPackage e.1 pkg = .ok escrow) :
    payoutCalls (tr.map fun e => e.2.2.2.2.2.2) ≤ 1 :=
  escrow_invokes_one_payout (deployed_ctx_lawful _) rfl h hpkg

end Nanodregg.Ledger.Proofs

