+CRIT-SPEC-2 — 2026-10-27 — outside statement review by Codex.
+
+The three apex statements are prose commitments, not theorems of the deployed processes. SPEC-19 admits that distinction. Several new component theorems close specific holes in the first review. The original continuity, intent, crash, contextual-semantics, and foreign-validity objections remain open.
+
+I read CRIT-SPEC.md before reviewing the source. I fetched origin/main on hbox at 5c0c20550e1f9a64f922ee30f1656f66df1f2ccf. Spec/ and decisions/spec.md at that revision have no diff from 87a1eb1c. I read CHARTER.md, Spec/README.md, System.lean, Trivial.lean, Claims.lean, Member.lean, Deployed.lean, and SPEC-7 through SPEC-20. Member.lean and Deployed.lean are under Spec/Proofs. I inspected the new claim definitions and their supporting statements in Agree, Auth, AuthKernel, Bell, Cap, Durable, Durable2, Escrow, Exchange, Foreign, Law, Receipt, and Sign. I followed the relevant definitions into ObjectHistory, Rooted, Protocol.Foreign, Host.Follow, Host.Loop, Host.Main, and Host.Cli. I read the local TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md sections 7 and 9 and their referenced architectural prices in section 11. The specified local documents are absent at their /Users paths on hbox. I read those documents locally. All repository inspection took place over ssh. I ran no builds. This review assesses statements and source wiring, not a new execution of their proofs or binaries.
+
+OWED: continuity from the member's authorized start. OneHistory.member still concludes that there exist a start w₁ and an audited log to the endpoint. It still does not require w₁ to be the initial w₀. Start.genesis still accepts arbitrary genesis inputs without tying their origin to me.world. Start.pinned still omits the adoption guard and the authority-certificate check. The reset-to-unrelated-genesis countermodel still satisfies A1. I do not claim that it satisfies the enlarged collection of claims. D2 now rejects a reset emitted as an empty steps list. That rejection does not strengthen A1's statement. SPEC-19 calls the continuity repair an in-flight composition of existing lemmas. The price is credible for retaining the per-frame extension or guarded-adoption evidence. That composition alone does not establish member authorization of the adoption command.
+
+OWED: removal of relay-supplied consent. keyFaithful still requires only that a parsed adoption continuation name the trusted sponsor key. It does not require the member to have requested adoption. The pinned bytes still receive their pre-pin validity from the sponsor's trust role. The theorem remains generic over the pin digest d. A binding interpretation still requires the deployed digest's cryptographic assumption. Keeping typed command state inside follow addresses continuation substitution. The contract must also bind the initial authenticated command to that state. An opaque handle alone is not a theorem of member intent. The week quoted for command-state work prices a mechanism, not the full intent invariant.
+
+PARAPHRASED: A1's existing floor and inhabitance. floor_oneHistory_member still rejects a World with height greater than steps or the wrong origin. It does not test structurally valid rollback or unauthorized adoption. inhabited_audited still exhibits an audited rotate without a Reach execution from a member-chosen start. The new inhabited_agree_pageAbsorbs does exhibit a real follower step from genesis to the batch's next World. That answers the narrower missing example of page absorption. It does not repair the continuity quantifiers.
+
+PARAPHRASED: one history between members. OneHistory.pair remains equality of endpoint Worlds conditional on a common start, equal step counts, and equal chains. It still permits isolated divergent histories with unequal chains. The new ForkConsistent and ForkConsistent.sync are stronger than that lemma. They range over S.follow and conclude equality of log prefixes. The post theorem applies only when the compared position lies strictly after the follower's old position and no later than its new position. Its refusal arm carries an existential mismatch witness. The sync theorem applies only when a frame crosses the supplied anchor from below. Its conclusion excludes a different prefix at that anchor unless the listed hash inputs collide. These are actual comparison theorems over the deployed command step.
+
+OWED: the full comparison contract. Both new fork theorems assume folds from the same g. A1 still does not establish that common trusted start. The sync theorem assumes that the continuation contains the intended anchor. It does not authenticate the source of that anchor. Neither theorem covers an already-passed anchor or missing retained evidence. The post theorem excludes posts beyond the page's end. Host.Follow.behindOr returns the original output when its retained-chain lookup produces no comparison. That behavior is not an explicit successful consistency proof. The post theorem also permits the page to be retained before the mismatch is reported. Its mathematical witness is not an exported certificate accusing the writer. Command.refused drops the post's step and member information from the reply bytes. A member attestation is not automatically attributable writer equivocation. SPEC-19 names these trust cases but supplies no distinct price for completing them. The older section 11.1 estimate starts from the stale assertion that members never compare heads. It does not price the remaining work.
+
+ANSWERED: finite page-acceptance completeness at the command-step boundary. Agree.pageAbsorbs proves that a bounded batch's staged events move the deployed follower to the batch's resulting World when supplied as the specified page. The premises require canonical rows, a canonical call codec, the same starting World, and a phase-2 continuation without an anchor. The page's events, chain, endpoint, and tip are supplied by equalities to the staged batch. The deaf follower is refuted by this statement. The deployed rotate page inhabits it. This is the finite input-output theorem requested in the first review. The proof does not require a liveness promise.
+
+OWED: delivery of that honest page by the deployed writer. Agree.pageAbsorbs does not prove that the pager reads the records staged by admitBatch. It does not establish persistence of the batch or delivery of a sequence of pages. SPEC-20 names the pager gap explicitly. The gap belongs in the Store trace and recovery work. Calling the premise “the writer's page” must not hide that missing composition.
+
+ANSWERED: the objection to an endpoint-only authority predicate. Integrity.authority quantifies over StepAt at each position of the actual audited log. It covers a changed cell that was live immediately before that step. A change followed by restoration remains covered. Deletion of a previously live cell remains covered. The object floor exhibits a non-owner write against this deployed claim. The first review's endpoint objection does not apply to this landed statement.
+
+PARAPHRASED: authority confinement for every accepted effect. Births are outside Integrity.authority. Nested authorization is stated at the dispatched actor without a composed account of that actor's authority back to the signed request. Several Authorized constructors leave the resulting cell unrestricted. Authorized.account takes an arbitrary after. AccountMove identifies a transfer or credit leg and its authority without equating the resulting balance change to the requested amount. A transfer of 1 is not required by this predicate to change balances by 1. Authorized.member identifies an authorized rotate without specifying the resulting member cell. The aggregate value theorem does not supply those missing local postconditions. SPEC-19's claim that the authority and value clauses compose today is too broad. Its “weeks per clause” price omits these effect-correspondence obligations.
+
+PARAPHRASED: value integrity. Integrity.value still proves aggregate balance conservation and conditional floor conservation. The floor exception still tests membership in createdIds rather than an independently stated issuance rule. w.Canon remains a premise. The top-level member-start predicate does not establish it for all allowed pins. The minting floor still tests the balance clause. The existing issue/account/transfer witness still demonstrates real movement rather than authority confinement. The first review's distinction between a legitimate mutation endpoint and a vacuous premise remains unchanged.
+
+ANSWERED: the absence of a separate deployed grant-cap theorem. Integrity.grantCap now bounds the selected transfer amounts and credit amounts along the system's own step chain. CellsCanon and LedgerContiguous are explicit premises. The capless transfer and credit floors exercise both ledgers. The formal quantification selects at most one dispatched amount per step. Reading this as the total of every nested move requires the accompanying uniqueness or aggregation argument. The README's claim that every follower-reached World supplies LedgerContiguous also needs qualification by the trusted-start case. A lemma about honest extensions does not establish the invariant for arbitrary pinned bytes.
+
+ANSWERED: a deployed contract for a successful two-party fuse. Integrity.exchange relates the admitted fuse to both offer legs and their destination accounts through EscrowLegMoved. The unfair-leg floor tests the agreement with the offers. This is a concrete local postcondition stronger than aggregate conservation. It does not state the behavior of every withdrawal or every take. It does not compose the exchange with command intent or crash recovery.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the new one-party escrow promise. Integrity.escrow bounds payoutCalls in a supplied ObjectHistory by one. It counts returned transfer calls without grants. It does not directly state a unique durable payment of the agreed amount to the agreed beneficiary. The program-equality premise fixes the language interpretation. The history premise supplies the operational execution. Every recorded judged entry must have e.2.1 = some pkg and read that package as S.escrow. ObjectHistory's write and law entries use none in that position. Those entries are therefore excluded by this premise. The README's “any history” sentence omits that restriction. A theorem that all relevant deployed escrow histories meet the premise is needed for that reading. The two-payout floor and one-payout witness address the stated counting claim. Neither artifact supplies the missing history-to-log and payment-effect composition.
+
+PARAPHRASED: B3's connection to the actual log. SPEC-17 strengthens the chain clause from some ObjectHistory to every ObjectHistory between the endpoints. This removes the particular escape of choosing a law-free alternative history. The relation still has no log parameter. Its constructors still call concrete receive and World.admit rather than S.receive and S.admit. The new universal quantifier therefore does not assert that the steps of l have the stated reference meaning. The existence conjunct still supplies only an endpoint history. SPEC-19 now names both the log-indexing repair and the system-field repair. It does not give a distinct price for them. Describing the universal form as already stating what a log-indexed history would state is false.
+
+OWED: applicable laws over their actual periods of applicability. B3 still requires the selected law to be in force at every recorded entry of the supplied history. It still requires the same package reading at every entry. It still begins from an object already live at w with a decodable initial state. Objects born inside l need a suffix composition. Laws added or removed during l need a per-step statement covering the actual old and incoming laws. These obligations are not named separately in SPEC-19's price. The broader independent-transition work must include them.
+
+OWED: contextual reference meaning and metering adequacy. Lang.NoRef still excludes hole-bearing laws. The chain conclusion contains no resource budget or charged-cost relation. It supplies no sufficient-fuel completeness result. It supplies no agreement result across sufficient fuel choices. It does not give the actual audited log's returned calls and relation edits their independent meaning. An evaluator that refuses all such computations is not excluded by one-way soundness. SPEC-19 names contextual adequacy and the NoRef lift. “Weeks per clause” is an estimate without a defined completion statement. Section 11.6 also retains the alternative of leaving contextual packages outside the promise. That alternative cannot complete the adopted second apex sentence, which expressly includes contextual programs.
+
+ANSWERED: the missing law-chain floor and useful law witness. floor_integrity_lawChain now rejects an unjudged write of n = 9 under bounded n 5. inhabited_law exhibits a deployed judged write of n = 3 with changed state. These statements address the first review's specific objection to using package mutation as evidence of sensitivity to a skipped law check. They do not establish contextual execution or metering adequacy.
+
+OWED: success for the member's actual command in the retained resulting World. Consent.okCommitted still extracts c from the last accumulator item of a phase-4 continuation. It still concludes a journal entry in f.world rather than f'.world. argv remains unrelated to c. The fabricated-continuation and destructive-success objections remain defects of this predicate. SPEC-19 explicitly promises the f'.world repair. The source still lacks it. The price of that repair is a small statement-and-proof composition. The price of intent correspondence includes the whole authenticated command lifecycle.
+
+PARAPHRASED: signing as consent. Consent.signs now relates a call-domain signing request to Command.body, signedReads, and plan on f'.world. It also concludes that the planned Turn is admissible there. This excludes merely passing the host's call bytes to custody. The command still comes from an unauthenticated continuation. u.member = me.member identifies a member field rather than a member authorization event. signShapes constrains the other message domains without proving authorization of every invitation, attestation, or cancellation. Command.start remains outside System. The new claims do not establish that the member intended the command eventually reported successful.
+
+PARAPHRASED: final settlement and stale finality as a consent contract. Consent.final still forbids later admission of the same call on an extension of the same World under RefusalsCanon and JournalCanon. It does not classify the truth of the refusal reason. It does not exclude prior admission, sibling-fork admission, or another call for the same intent. Consent.staleFinal still assumes the plan, signed reads, canonical structures, and two folds. Its cmd and body b still lack the body-from-command equation. The stale floor still combines signed reads for one command with a separately planned body. The new signing theorem contains a body equation but does not connect that signing step to this settlement step. The expired witness does not establish a complete stale command execution. These are unchanged branch-local results. Their composition with intent and recovery is owed under the quarter-scale plan.
+
+OWED: an authorized cancellation record connected to subsequent non-admission. Consent.cancelOk still concludes only that S.settle returns cancelled on f'.world. It does not name the recorded cancellation. Coupling a successful follower to an always-cancelled settlement still demonstrates the statement's dependence on its own settlement field. SPEC-19 promises the record clause without landing it. The small composition price is honest for exposing the record. It does not cover command provenance or crash survival. ANSWERED: the specific absence of a deployed successful cancellation witness. inhabited_cancelOk now exhibits the settle step at a World with the founder's recorded cancellation. It remains a prepared-state step rather than a complete authorized command journey.
+
+ANSWERED: the request to demote Shown.onlyFollowers. The README places it outside the top-level contract. top_shown remains the deployed handler theorem about defer authorization. The revised description explicitly excludes reply and lookup disclosures. No confidentiality theorem has replaced it. TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md section 8 still says an unauthenticated party learns nothing. The correction inserted into section 7 does not make that older sentence true. The trusted-operator boundary must be expressed consistently wherever this claim is advertised.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the claim that floors finish the vacuity obligation. New floors now exercise unauthorized writes, skipped law checks, deaf page absorption, fork blindness, and false receipt answers. Those additions improve discrimination against the named failures. They do not establish adequacy of every conjunct. Claims.lean still calls the floors the tree's whole vacuity obligation. TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md section 1.3 still treats refuting one system as the whole obligation. The first review rejected that inference. A positive witness for one phase still does not establish command provenance. A NoRef write still does not inhabit contextual execution. The required remedy is a correctly scoped contract with the completeness properties it promises. It is not a plant for every internal lemma.
+
+PARAPHRASED: writer durability. Durable.reopen proves that a successful open folds all records recognized by Log.recover past a Kept base. Its completeness clause rejects the old amnesiac-open countermodel. Its recovered World is Kept. The records are still defined through the same recovery procedure. No acknowledgment appears in the claim. No crash transition relates the supplied bytes to a previous execution. No Audited conclusion carries the writer's decision provenance through recovery. SPEC-11 names these missing connections. The section 11.4 effect-and-crash work is the right kind of price. The quote of one month in SPEC-19 is not the same scope as section 11.4's “a month plus a quarter.”
+
+PARAPHRASED: follower durability. Durable.follower states that a returned .steps ts is a Rooted run of S.step from f.world to f'.world. “Persists” here means “returns persistence instructions.” It does not mean that storage has executed them. The predicate ignores whether r acknowledges a command. It contains no durable state, synchronization event, crash, or reopen. Rooted establishes admission and the per-step roots notes. It does not by itself establish audited derivation. The .adopt branch lies outside the claim. A loop that sends the result and drops the returned steps leaves this theorem intact. This is not acknowledged-implies-persisted durability.
+
+OWED: follower acknowledgment durability. Host.Follow.loop visibly calls append before port.send in its nonempty-steps success arm. The adoption arm similarly waits for ck.adopt. That source ordering is evidence about the implementation. top_durable_follower does not prove its crash consequence. A proper theorem must connect the earlier steps already retained by follow to a later settle-only acknowledgment. It must cover adoption and failures as well as nonempty appends. It must preserve the acknowledged audited prefix on reopen while permitting a durable unacknowledged suffix. Durable2's header names the ordering gap honestly. Its claim that the order is unstatable because the loop uses IO overstates the obstacle. The missing item is an effect semantics and a proved connection to the executed loop.
+
+ANSWERED: resident keep atomicity under the declared volume model. Durable.resident states old-or-new contents after a crash at any prefix of keep. It states the new contents after a completed keep. It states preservation of the readable entry through durableResumeVia. Stable, Fresh, and Live supply the prefix clause's volume invariants. Crash supplies the allowed persistence outcomes. The in-place-keep floor rejects loss of both old and new contents. The equality to deployed resumeOps connects the resume recipe. This theorem is about a kept text under that volume contract. It is not an end-to-end theorem about a ring's accepted answer or external charge.
+
+OWED: the Bell lifecycle. Bell.ringer constrains one returning method run against the caller's pinned waiting row. Bell.oneAsk constrains effect paths and within-process tick chains. The second clause allows a later spent ask after an intervening Frees event. Neither theorem follows one ring life through World admission, persistent intent, provider charge, answer persistence, lost replies, and restart. Durable.resident does not perform that composition. SPEC-15 correctly rejects “once per ring key” for the current reuse behavior. SPEC-19 names RingLife and prices the composite under the longer intent-and-crash work. That is an honest scope statement only if provider-boundary charging and the persistent broker cap are included. They cannot be replaced by a local ask counter. The predicate must distinguish a request not sent from a request sent with its reply lost.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the foreign-receipt finding. ReceiptTruth is a new theorem about checking a receipt against the roots and height of a supplied folded World. Its committed arm establishes a journal entry. Its refused arm establishes a recorded refusal. Its absent arm establishes absence of both entries. Each arm exposes the relevant collision alternative. The fold-from-genesis premise supplies the representation invariants. This answers the local receipt-content question. It establishes neither the validity nor the authority of a foreign head. It does not prove the semantic truth of a recorded refusal reason. It does not establish source-call authorization from a bare fold.
+
+OWED: what a foreign receipt can establish for its recipient. Foreign still assumes both a fold from genesis and HeadOf. HeadOf supplies equality of the held roots to that folded World's roots. The conclusion finds a Turn admitted in that fold. It does not require Audited. Protocol.Foreign.check does not invoke certificate verification. Its head match ignores the stored origin and chain. It checks the receipt against the supplied height and roots. The theorem therefore does not authenticate a binding from the claimed source World to a trusted authority. ReceiptTruth is not a substitute for that binding. Adding a verify-receipt command would expose the checker to a user. It would not establish foreign validity or authority.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the claimed correction from validity to accountability. SPEC-19 and the correction in TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md section 9 state the distinction correctly. A signature on a fabricated head remains compatible with signing at most one head per step. The foreign contract must choose re-execution, a checkpoint trusted for validity, or an independently authenticated authority assumed to certify valid extensions. That choice is still prose. No new Spec theorem connects the foreign receipt path to such a choice. No new Spec theorem supplies attributable equivocation evidence from that path. The README's HeadOf premise entry still says writer-signed heads delete it. Foreign.lean's header still says HeadOf reduces to signing at most one head per step. Section 9 retains the same false discharge immediately after its correction. These are contradictory specifications of the missing guarantee. The older weeks-and-re-genesis price buys signed endorsements. It does not buy validity. SPEC-19 gives the alternatives but no price for the selected validity architecture because no architecture is selected.
+
+OWED: an independent semantic boundary. Audited still means agreement with the supplied kernel's own decisions. Authorized and the new component claims add independently readable constraints on parts of those decisions. They do not form the adopted independent transition relation. ObjectHistory and Dispatched still bring operational implementation meanings into the review boundary. Proof scripts can remain untrusted. Definitions determining the proposition cannot become unread merely by living in another file. SPEC-19 accepts a relation over observations and authority effects as the remedy. TOPLEVEL-SPEC.md section 1.1 still rejects an abstract model as a duplicate implementation. Section 11 still claims that any meaning change below a claim becomes a build error. Weakening a definition can preserve an existing proof. Those older methodological claims remain false.
+
+OWED: deployment wiring. System remains a record of independent component functions. Updating receive does not make follow call the replacement. Updating admit does not make submit call the replacement. Host.Main delegates to Host.Cli.main. The serve and follow branches assemble their loops from concrete definitions outside Spec.System. A theorem that main calls the record's handle and follow fields would close the selection-drift gap for those calls. It would not establish that the selected follow uses the selected kernel fields. It would not establish any ordering between a durable append and an observable reply. It would not establish refinement to the independent contract.
+
+The closure criterion is a theorem about the actual program's observable traces under named effect contracts. That theorem must follow the real entry-point dispatch, startup and recovery paths, loop state, calls, and effects. One implementation route is to assemble the callers from shared primitive operations in a module used by production. A second route is to retain concrete callers and prove their connection to the execution relation. Making main take System is not intrinsically required by either route. A proof-facing record of unrelated functions is not sufficient for either route. An effect program interpreted by both production IO and the crash model would make this connection easier to maintain. A small interpreter's conformance would then be an explicit proof obligation or trusted boundary. Compiler, FFI, filesystem, transport, and custody assumptions must be stated at their actual interfaces.
+
+SPEC-19 prices the entry-point change at days and the effect interpreter at a month. Days is a plausible scope for replacing duplicated selection sites with shared assembly. It is not a price for proving the composed system. The durable trace also needs pager provenance, checkpoint and adoption behavior, restart invariants, asynchronous sealing, and observable failure outcomes. The entry-point theorem and those semantic obligations need separate completion criteria. Treating their sum as a wiring theorem underprices the gap.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the first adopted apex. It is prose in SPEC-19 and the README. No Lean declaration states observable-execution refinement to an intent-indexed durable contract. Its strongest available support consists of separate signing, settlement, page-agreement, recovery, volume, and Bell claims. No composition relates their events to one execution or one intent. Continuation decoding and planning supply the command for the consent slices. Kept and the recovery procedure supply the recovered-state interpretation. Stable, Fresh, Live, and Crash supply resident persistence. Runs and TicksWith supply the Bell effect histories. These premises do the work that an actual process invariant must eventually discharge. The quarter-scale price names the right major work areas but does not replace the missing trace relation.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the second adopted apex. It is prose rather than a theorem about every accepted transition. The strongest support combines per-step live-cell authority, aggregate value conservation, selected grant spending, package-specific exchange and escrow properties, and NoRef law chains. Audited supplies kernel decision provenance. Canonical-state and ledger-contiguity premises supply the representation invariants. Dispatched supplies nested operational provenance. NoRef and the every-entry in-force premise restrict the semantic law conclusion. Program equality fixes the exchange and escrow interpreter. Births, exact local effects, changing laws, contextual evaluation, and resource adequacy remain outside the composed promise. Weeks per clause is not an honest completion price until those clauses are stated.
+
+PARAPHRASED: the third adopted apex. It is prose rather than a theorem covering all accepted views and foreign receipts. The strongest member result is still endpoint recoverability from some Start under keyFaithful. The strongest comparison results now identify equal prefixes at an in-page comparison. The strongest foreign result assumes the folded source World and its root binding. The common g, the crossed-position inequalities, and HeadOf supply the missing continuity and source-history connections. The collision alternatives expose cryptographic exceptions without authenticating authority. None of these statements supplies the advertised combination of an explicitly trusted start and checkable writer equivocation evidence.
+
+Before calling the top-level spec complete, I require formal versions of all three apex contracts with the deployed-process refinement proved. The intent contract must follow one authenticated intent through retries, signing, settlement, cancellation, persistence, and restart. The transition contract must cover births, exact authorized effects, issuance, delegated spending, applicable contextual laws, and the declared resource budget. The view contract must retain the authorized start and distinguish verified comparison, unavailable evidence, and attributable equivocation. Foreign receipts must use an explicitly selected validity source with an independently authenticated authority binding. The Bell must instantiate the durable intent contract over a ring life at the provider's charging boundary. Every surviving explanatory sentence must agree with those theorem statements.
+
+The project is closer to a reviewable trusted statement than it was in the first review because it now exposes more component obligations, but completion still requires the three apex refinement theorems over the actual deployed executions.

